<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>36: Laurent series</title>
  <introduction>
    <p><md>
<mrow>e^u \amp  = \amp  1 + u + \frac{u^2}{2!} + \frac{u^3}{3!} + \ldots +
          \frac{u^n}{n!} + \ldots</mrow>
<mrow>\sin(u) \amp  = \amp  u - \frac{u^3} {3!} + \frac{u^5}{5!} - \frac{u^7}{7!} +
\ldots + (-1)^n\frac{u^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!} + \ldots</mrow>
<mrow>\cos(u) \amp  = \amp  1 - \frac{u^2}{2!} + \frac{u^4}{4!} - \frac{u^6}{6!} +
\ldots + (-1)^n\frac{u^{2n}}{(2n)!} + \ldots</mrow>
</md></p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the Laurent series for the function <m>f(z) =
  \sin\left(\frac2z\right)</m> around the point <m>z_0 = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is <m>\res_0 f(z)</m>?
  What is <m>\oint_C f(z)~dz</m> if <m>C</m> is the unit circle?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that <m>f(z) = \frac{\cos(3z) - 1}{z^2}</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find its
  Laurent series about the point <m>z_0 = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the residue <m>\res_0 f(z)</m>?
  What is <m>\oint_C f(z)~dz</m> if <m>C</m> is the unit circle?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace=".8in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that <m>f(z) = \frac{\sin(2z^2)-2z^2}{z^{11}}</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find its
  Laurent series about the point <m>z_0 = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the residue <m>\res_0 f(z)</m>?
  What is <m>\oint_C f(z)~dz</m> if <m>C</m> is the unit circle?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>There are three types of singularities that can be classified by
  their Laurent series:
  <md>
  f(z) = \ldots + \frac{c_{-2}}{(z-z_0)^2} + \frac{c_{-1}}{z-z_0} +
  c_0 + c_1 (z-z_0) + \ldots.
  </md></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><term>Removable singularity:</term>  <m>c_k = 0</m> if <m>k&lt;0</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Pole:</term> There are only finitely many nonzero <m>c_k</m> with
    <m>k</m> negative.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Essential singularity:</term> There are infinitely many nonzero
    <m>c_k</m> with <m>k</m> negative.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace=".8in">
      <statement>
        <p>The first three examples illustrate each of these types.  Which is
  which?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The <em>order</em> of a pole is the smallest positive integer <m>M</m> where
  <m>c_k = 0</m> if <m>k &lt; -M</m>.  What is the order of the pole from the first
  three examples?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>f(z) = \frac{1}{z^2+1}</m>.  Find the singularities,
  determine their types, and find their residues.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
